course: dictionary
lesson: 5687
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, so context tells you which one is meant. As a noun, a lock is a device on a door, box or bike that keeps it closed until you use a key, code or card. As a verb, to lock is to close something with a lock. Read the words around the word to decide.
explain: «Ransomware is criminal software that locks files and demands payment.» The clues are files and payment. Here lock is a verb: the software shuts the files so you cannot open them. Check by swapping in your meaning: "closes the files so you cannot use them" still makes sense.
explain: «To take a boat up through a lock, the bottom gates are shut and water flows in.» The clues are boat, gates and water. This lock is a section of canal with gates where boats are raised or lowered. Compare this with a lock of hair, which is just a small bunch of hair. Same spelling, different meaning.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: Lock can be a noun (a lock on a door) or a verb (to lock the door).
fact: A lock of hair is a small bunch of hair, and a lock on a canal is a section with gates for boats.
fact: Useful phrases: «lock up» means lock all the doors, «locked out» means unable to get in, and «under lock and key» means kept safely locked.
exercise: «Bats sleep hanging upside down with their foot tendons locked.» What does locked mean here? | Sleepy and slow | Opened wide | *Held tight in place, so they stay put | Stretched out to fly
exercise: «Ransomware is criminal software that locks files and demands payment.» What does locks files mean? | Copies the files onto a memory stick | Opens the files wide | Sends the files to school | *Makes the files impossible to open until payment is made
exercise: Grandma says, «She had a lock of hair across her forehead.» What is a lock here? | *A small bunch of hair | A door fastening | A key | A gate on a canal
test: «Lock up the shed before you go to bed.» What does lock up mean? | Lift the shed off the ground | Paint the shed | Sleep in the shed | *Close all the doors with a lock
test: A boat rises through a lock on the canal. What is a lock here? | A device that keeps a bike closed | *A section with gates where boats are raised or lowered | A bunch of hair | A type of key
exam: «She was locked out of the house after forgetting her key.» What does locked out mean? | *Unable to get in | Shut inside a fridge | Holding a small bunch of hair | Safely kept in a box
